% c411: second idea, first minim of each phrase (45, 49, 121, 125, 277, 281): dotted crotchet + passing quaver inside the minim (d'''4. c'''8 b''4~ was d'''2 b''4~), filling the chordal third before the rising sixth. The tie over the bar line and the three-minim hemiola stay (attacks still on beats 1, 3, 2); the quaver is unaccented, as the lead-on quaver at 48/52 (Sebesky PDF 210, Ex. 277-278: a long-value lead line needs a figure of its own; PDF 206, Ex. 270: a recurring figure unifies).
% c218: 127-128 Vln I/II end the slur before the quaver (b''4.) cis'''8), violas b4) e'2, so the hemiola 129-131 starts down-bow as at 285 (c085; reversed since c118's up-bow at 121); Adler PDF 38. 127 violas' repeated B as louré (Adler PDF 42).
% c143: theme A bar 10 (22, 114, 262): the bass neighbour A-G-A under iv becomes A-G sharp-A, V6 of iv (E major over G sharp) instead of i6 (A-S Unit 18 PDF 297: in minor the neighbouring I6 to IV is "generally altered to form an applied dominant, V6 of IV"; a bass neighbour works best by half step). The leading tone G sharp is only in the bass line (A-S PDF 293); inner parts take B or E. 314 is unchanged: its G is passing (F sharp-G-A, c058).
% c104: 119 Hn 2 F sharp 4 on beat 1 (was B3), paired with Tpt 2 F sharp 4: B3 made a major second with Hn 3-4 A3 inside the horn section at ff; Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 102-103 gives discords of the second to different colours, PDF 103-104 pairs horns with trumpets. c082 kept: still no D sharp under the tail E.
% c082: 119 Hn 2 B3, Tbn 2 B2 on beat 1, Tpt 2 motive x on F sharp 4 (were D sharp): the theme tail E is a 4-3 appoggiatura over V7, so the 3rd D sharp is not sounded under it (A-S PDF 474 Ex. 27-16a, PDF 469). 116 Hn 1 F sharp 4 on beat 1 (was B4 a semitone under the flat-9 C5). 126 Hn 2 E4-D sharp 4, Bsn 1 E3-F sharp 3: complete V flat 9, no inner 4th (A-S PDF 463, 465, 475).
% c085: 105 Vla "non div." on the off-beat double stops (Adler PDF 30: "it is safer to write non div."); 129-131 hemiola rebowed as 53-55.
% SCHERZO, reprise of the first part, bars 105-140.
% c022: 111 viola B+A, Hn 1 B, Hn 2 F sharp (no augmented 2nd C-D sharp); 125-126 bass C-B, Bsn 1 E-D sharp: iv6-V (Phrygian) instead of the augmented 2nd C-D sharp in the bass (A-S PDF 168-169, 171 Ex. 11-14).
% 105-120: theme A ff, tutti: Vln I with Vln II and Ob 1 at pitch, Cl 2 and the cor anglais an octave lower; Fl 1-2 enter an octave higher at 109 (c125); Vln II drops to the lower octave at 113 (c067); horns on beats 1 and 3, (c193: header brought up to date)
%   trumpets in motive x (a fanfare figure only: the trumpets have no melody in the scherzo; cyclic-notes), timpani with the bass.
% 121-132: the second idea in E minor (it was in G major at 45), hemiola.
% c063: 121 sub. p after the ff tutti, strings first (Fl 1 and Ob 1 rest 121-124 and enter 125), crescendo 125-128 to f at the hemiola cadence 129:
%   the restatement takes a new colour and level instead of a fourth loud arrival in a row (Adler II PDF 257-258 dialogue; 190 forte subito piano; 267 the
%   orchestra reduced when the solo line enters after the tutti; 288 the doubling changed in the second phrase). The 16-bar ff block 105-120 now gives way.
% 133-140: close: motive x in the cellos, dying away to E minor pp.
% c023: timpani E pp at 139 with the basses' E (was 138, under the cellos' V F sharp-D sharp-B).

% c036: 109-112 and 117-120 Fl 1 an octave above Fl 2, as at 309-312 and 317-320 in the reprise: two flutes in unison on E5/B4 add little in a ff tutti
% (Adler PDF 174, 185: 'sweet and with little carrying power'; the register from E6 is 'clear and brilliant'); 108 beat 3 rest for the leap, as at 308
% c052/c092: 105 the soprano seventh A5 of the V7 at 104 resolves by step in its own register: Cl 1 G5 on beat 1 (c065 had put Fl 1 back on E5 for clean flute part-combining, which left the A5 unresolved and made hidden octaves A5-E5 over B1-E1; Aldwell-Schachter PDF 76, 87-88). Flutes and Vln I leap to the theme's E5
%   (A-S PDF 406-407 sec. 9, Ex. 24-11: a transferred resolution needs the tone of resolution in the same register).
% c124: 105-108 the flutes rest and enter a2 at 109 in the octave above the violins (were a2 at pitch with Vln I, Vln II/Ob 1 on E5-B5 and B4, the flute's
%   weak low-middle register under ff brass: Adler I PDF 185). Adler II PDF 151 (Mozart 40/iv: every instrument placed where every note tells), PDF 152 (Beethoven 9/i:
%   the high flute octave carries alone), PDF 150 (Barber: the doubling grows with each statement), PDF 156 (a rest before a colour makes its entry heard).
%   The top octave cannot start at 105 (B6 at 107 is kept for the coda peak), so the first phrase is strings, oboe, clarinets and cor anglais. Cl 1 G5 still resolves the seventh (c092).
% c167: 125 Fl 1 and Ob 1 join the violins' second idea pp< (was p<), a level under the strings they double, and still reach f with them at 129 (Sebesky PDF 164: a flute added to the lead violin line thickens it; mark the wind softer than the strings).
% c368: 125 "espress." on Fl 1 and Ob 1 where they join the violins' second idea (as 45 and 277; MOLA PDF 8, Ex. 6).
sCFluteOne = { R2.*4 | e'''4-.\ff b''8-. b''-. e'''4-. | a'''2->( gis'''8 g'''8 | fis'''4-\sysLabel "a2"-.) dis'''8-. dis'''-. b''4-. | e'''4-. r4 r4 | a''4-. g''-. fis''-. | e''4-. dis''8-. dis''-. e''4-. | fis''4-.( g''8 a''8) b''4-. | c'''2->( b''8 fis''8) | e'''4-. b''8-. b''-. e'''4-. | g'''2->( fis'''8 f'''8 | e'''4-.) dis'''8-. dis'''-. fis'''4-. | e'''4-. r4 r4 | R2.*4 | c'''4.->(\pp^\markup \italic "espress."\< b''8 a''4~ | a''4 fis'''2 | e'''2 dis'''4~ | dis'''4 b''4. cis'''8) | b''4(\f g''4 e''4 | fis''2 dis''4 | fis''4-\sysLabel "1." e''2) | e''4-. r4 r4 | R2.*8 | }
sCFluteTwo = { R2.*4 | e'''4-.\ff b''8-. b''-. e'''4-. | a'''2->( gis'''8 g'''8 | fis'''4-.) dis'''8-. dis'''-. b''4-. | e'''4-. r4 r4 | a''4-. g''-. fis''-. | e''4-. dis''8-. dis''-. e''4-. | fis''4-.( g''8 a''8) b''4-. | c'''2->( b''8 fis''8) | e'''4-. b''8-. b''-. e'''4-. | g'''2->( fis'''8 f'''8 | e'''4-.) dis'''8-. dis'''-. fis'''4-. | e'''4-. r4 r4 | R2.*12 | R2.*8 | }  % c078: 109-112 and 117-120 Fl 2 joins Fl 1 in the upper octave (was the lower octave B4-A5 at ff, the flute's weak register under the tutti; Adler PDF 185)
% c135: "1." at 125 (Ob 1 alone after four bars rest; the part-combiner keeps its solo state through rests and prints no label; MOLA PDF 4, Ex. 6)
% c214: 132 Ob 1 E5 (was E4) on the closing crotchet, with Fl 1 and Vln I, the octave its line has held since 129: E4 was the only oboe note below F4 in the movement, the coarse lowest fifth, at f and doubling Vln II (Adler II PDF 151: Mozart 40/iv keeps the oboes off the low note they would make raucous and places every instrument where every note tells). As 288.
sCOboeOne = { \partCombineApart e''4-. b'8-. b'-. e''4-. | \partCombineSoloI g''2->( fis''8 f''8 | e''4-.) b''8-. b''-. g''4-. | fis''4-. dis''-. b'-. | e''4-. b'8-. b'-. e''4-. | a''2->( gis''8 g''8 | fis''4-\sysLabel "1."-.) dis''8-. dis''-. b'4-. | e''4-. r4 r4 | a''4-. g''-. fis''-. | e''4-. dis''8-. dis''-. e''4-. | fis''4-.( g''8 a''8) b''4-. | c'''2->( b''8 fis''8) | e''4-. b'8-. b'-. e''4-. | g''2->( fis''8 f''8 | e''4-.) dis''8-. dis''-. fis''4-. | e''4-. r4 r4 | \partCombineAutomatic R2.*4 | c''4.->(\pp\<^\markup \bold "1."^\markup \italic "espress." b'8 a'4~ | a'4 fis''2 | e''2 dis''4~ | dis''4 b'4. cis''8) | b'4(\f g''4 e''4 | fis''2 dis''4 | fis''4-\sysLabel "1." e''2) | e''4-. r4 r4 | R2.*8 | }
% c189: 105 Ob 2 E5 on beat 1 (was G4 on beat 2): since c184 Ob 2 holds the leading tone D#5 at 101-104, not the seventh A4 that c057's G4 resolved; the leading tone now rises to E5 in its own voice (A-S PDF 374-375: the leading tone moves up to the tonic) instead of leaping an augmented fifth down after a rest.
sCOboeTwo = { \partCombineApart e''4-. r4 \tag #'score \once \override Rest.staff-position = #-6 r4 | \partCombineSoloI R2.*15 | \partCombineAutomatic R2. | R2.*11 | R2.*8 | }
% c021: the cor anglais doubles Cl 2's lower octave of theme A (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 66: the clarinet is weak in its middle compass; PDF 68: Ob / Eng. horn in octaves always good)
% c094: 133-136 (and 321-323) cor anglais B3 and Cl 2 E4 exchanged: the clarinet pair lies over the cor anglais and bassoon in normal order, as at 269-270, instead of enclosing the cor anglais (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 91-93).
% c224: 133-136 C.a. holds B4 (was B3, written F#4, the top of its lowest fifth, where no soft dying close can be asked of it; Adler I PDF 198, 205): Adler II PDF 159,
%   'assign pitches to the instruments in their best registral positions so that the notes may be performed at the desired dynamic'. Same layout as 269-271 (c221): C.a. over the clarinet pair over Bsn 1.
% c288: 133-136 (and 321-323 in dC) the winds' fading E-minor chord slurred with tenuto (soft 'da' tonguing): unmarked, each bar was a new tongued attack at p
%   that undid the diminuendo, while the cellos' motive x dies away under it (Adler I PDF 309, 321; Ex. X-16 PDF 306-307, held horn harmony under slurs).
sCCorAnglais = { e'4-. b8-. b-. e'4-. | g'2->( fis'8 f'8 | e'4-.) b'8-. b'-. g'4-. | fis'4-. dis'-. b-. | e'4-. b8-. b-. e'4-. | a'2->( gis'8 g'8 | fis'4-.) dis'8-. dis'-. b4-. | e'4-. r4 r4 | a'4-. g'-. fis'-. | e'4-. dis'8-. dis'-. e'4-. | fis'4-.( g'8 a'8) b'4-. | c''2->( b'8 fis'8) | e'4-. b8-. b-. e'4-. | g'2->( fis'8 f'8 | e'4-.) dis'8-. dis'-. fis'4-. | e'4-. r4 r4 | R2.*12 | g'2.--(\p | g'2.-- | g'2.--\> | g'2.--)\! | R2.*4 | }
% c102: 123-124 (and 279-280) the passing bass B-A-G under the second idea is V - V4/2 - i6: Cl 2 D sharp 4, Cl 1, Hn 2 and violas hold F sharp 4 to the bar's second minim (was an A-major triad, IV after V and before i6; Aldwell-Schachter PDF 135, 148 rules 1-3, 142 Ex. 9-14). Melody unchanged; its C sharp is a passing tone.
sCClOne = { \partCombineApart g''4-. r4 r4 | \partCombineSoloII R2.*15 | \partCombineAutomatic g'2(\subP b'4~ | b'4 g'2) | fis'2.~( | fis'4 g'2) | c''4.(\< b'8 a'4~ | a'2.) | g'2( fis'4~ | fis'4 e'2) | g'4(\f g' g' | a'2.~ | a'4 g'2) | g'4-. r4 r4 | b'2.--(\p | b'2.-- | b'2.--\> | b'2.--)\! | R2.*4 | }
